  "statement": "Let $X_0/\\mathbf F_q$ be smooth, proper and geometrically connected of pure\ndimension $d$. With $P_i$ and $b_i$ as in [sk-thm-intfactors]:\n\n1. $Z(X_0,T)=\\prod_{i=0}^{2d}P_i(T)^{(-1)^{i+1}}$ is rational;\n   each $P_i$ has integer coefficients and constant coefficient one.\n1. Each reciprocal root $\\alpha$ of $P_i$ satisfies\n   $|\\tau(\\alpha)|=q^{i/2}$ for every complex embedding $\\tau$.\n1. $b_i=b_{2d-i}$, and these numbers are independent of $\\ell$.\n   Moreover $P_0=1-T$, $P_{2d}=1-q^dT$, and $b_0=b_{2d}=1$.\n1. Put $\\chi=\\sum_i(-1)^ib_i$. Then $d\\chi$ is even and there\n   is $\\varepsilon\\in\\{1,-1\\}$ such that\n\n$$\nZ\\!\\left(X_0,\\frac1{q^dT}\\right)\n =\\varepsilon q^{d\\chi/2}T^\\chi Z(X_0,T)\n \\quad\\hbox{in }\\mathbf Q(T).\n$$\n\n*Complement.* The map $\\alpha\\mapsto q^d/\\alpha$ carries the reciprocal roots\nof $P_i$ bijectively, with multiplicities, onto those of $P_{2d-i}$.",
  "proof": [
   {
    "text": "The first assertion follows from [sk-cor-tf-point-counting] and\n[sk-thm-intfactors], and the second is the purity\nstatement for all complex embeddings in that theorem.\nFrobenius-equivariant Poincaré duality, already used in\n[sk-cor-tf-functional-equation], matches the eigenvalues in\ndegrees $i$ and $2d-i$ as $\\alpha$ and $q^d/\\alpha$, and hence\n$b_i=b_{2d-i}$. Independence of $\\ell$ follows from $b_i=\\deg P_i$.",
    "uses": [
     "sk-thm-intfactors"
    ],
    "elementary": false
   },
   {
    "text": "Since $X$ is connected, a global section of its constant sheaf is\nconstant, so $H^0(X,\\Ql)=\\Ql$ with Frobenius acting as the identity.\nThe trace map and duality identify $H^{2d}(X,\\Ql)$ with $\\Ql(-d)$\n([sk-cor-trace]), on which Frobenius acts by $q^d$. This proves the\nassertions in degrees $0$ and $2d$; when $d=0$, the two descriptions concern\nthe same group.",
    "uses": [
     "sk-cor-trace"
    ],
    "elementary": false
   },
   {
    "text": "For the functional equation, apply\n[sk-cor-tf-functional-equation] with\n$D=\\prod_i\\det(F_i)^{(-1)^i}$. It shows that $D^2=q^{d\\chi}$ and that\nthe coefficient of $T^\\chi$ is $(-1)^\\chi D$.\nThe equalities $b_i=b_{2d-i}$ give $\\chi\\equiv b_d\\pmod2$. When $d$ is\nodd, graded commutativity makes the perfect pairing on the middle\ncohomology alternating, so $b_d$ is even (the complement of\n[sk-cor-tf-functional-equation]). Thus $d\\chi$ is always even.\nPut $\\varepsilon=(-1)^\\chi Dq^{-d\\chi/2}$, whose square is one.\nThe resulting formula is an identity in $\\mathbf Q(T)$, because the\nzeta function is rational over $\\mathbf Q$ and the scalar\n$\\varepsilon q^{d\\chi/2}$ is a rational number.",
    "uses": [],
    "elementary": null
   },
   {
    "text": "The complement is the eigenvalue matching above, transported to the $P_i$ by\n$P_i=P_{i,\\ell}$.",
    "uses": [],
    "elementary": null
   }
  ],
  "note": "the current blueprint (main-20261025, weil-conjectures-blueprint b767183f5c) states or proves this differently; a worker who touches the node follows it"
 }
